* t55strap.a -- CONF68K test t55: an F$STrap handler is entered with the* registers the manual lists.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): when an error exception reaches a routine* installed with F$STrap, the routine is passed d7.w = the exception vector* offset, (a0) = the program counter when the exception occurred -- the same* value as R$PC(a5) -- (a1) = the stack pointer when it occurred, R$a7(a5),* (a5) = the register image, and (a6) = the primary global data pointer.** A handler written to that contract restores its registers from (a5) and* jumps through a0. GNU Oleo's run-time is one: it saves a0, handles the* exception, restores the image and returns through the saved a0. A system* that leaves a0 unset resumes such a program at whatever a0 happened to* hold.** The test installs a TRAPV handler, sets V and executes TRAPV. The handler* checks three of the five registers against the image it was given and* reports; it does not resume. The observed value is a bit set of what* disagreed -- 1 for a0, 2 for a1, 4 for d7 -- so zero is the documented* behaviour and any other value names the register at fault. a0 is zero* when TRAPV executes, so a system that does not set it cannot pass by* coincidence. If TRAPV comes back without reaching the handler the test* reports 8.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1*FSTrap set $0ET_TRAPV set $1CRPC set $42RA7 set $3CVBIT set 2*EXPECT set 0* psect t55strap,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart moveq #0,d0 movea.l d0,a0 lea exctbl(pc),a1 trap #0 dc.w FSTrap bcs.w setuperr movea.l d0,a0 ori #VBIT,ccr trapv moveq #8,d6 bra.w isfail** The table sits BEFORE the handler, so its offset is positive. That keeps* this test about the registers alone: whether a negative offset reaches* its routine is a separate question.exctbl dc.w T_TRAPV,handler-*-4 dc.w -1*handler moveq #0,d6 cmpa.l RPC(a5),a0 beq.s chka1 addq.l #1,d6chka1 cmpa.l RA7(a5),a1 beq.s chkd7 addq.l #2,d6chkd7 cmpi.w #T_TRAPV,d7 beq.s judge addq.l #4,d6judge cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.w ispass bra.w isfail* use verdict.i use report.i*idstr dc.b "RESULT t55 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  an F$STrap handler gets the PC in a0, SP in a1, vector in d7",0 ends